"""Printed 293–295: homogeneous reduction and complete bacterial example."""
from math12_reviewed_unit1 import heading,paragraph,page
from math12_reviewed_higher_derivatives import eq,note
EX9=[heading('Example 9'),paragraph('Find the general solution of the homogeneous differential equation dy/dx=(y−x)/(y+x).'),heading('Solution'),paragraph('The given differential equation defines a homogeneous function of degree zero on its right side:'),eq(r'\frac{\lambda y-\lambda x}{\lambda y+\lambda x}=\frac{\lambda(y-x)}{\lambda(y+x)}=\lambda^{1-1}\frac{y-x}{y+x}=\lambda^0\frac{y-x}{y+x}=\frac{y-x}{y+x},\quad\mathrm{HDE}'),paragraph('Use y=ux and dy/dx=u+x du/dx to obtain a separable differential equation:'),eq(r'u+x\frac{du}{dx}=\frac{ux-x}{ux+x}=\frac{u-1}{u+1}'),eq(r'x\frac{du}{dx}=\frac{u-1}{u+1}-u=\frac{u-1-u^2-u}{u+1}=-\frac{u^2+1}{u+1}','i'),eq(r'-\frac{u+1}{u^2+1}\,du=\frac{dx}{x},\quad\mathrm{SDE}','ii'),paragraph('Integrating the general solution; multiply and divide the u term by 2:'),eq(r'-\int\frac{(u+1)du}{u^2+1}=\int\frac{dx}{x}\quad\Rightarrow\quad-\int\frac{2(u+1)du}{2(u^2+1)}=\int\frac{dx}{x}'),eq(r'-\frac12\int\frac{2u\,du}{u^2+1}-\int\frac{du}{u^2+1}=\ln x+c'),eq(r'-\frac12\ln(u^2+1)-\tan^{-1}u=\ln x+\ln c'),eq(r'-\ln\sqrt{u^2+1}-\tan^{-1}u=\ln(cx)'),eq(r'-\tan^{-1}u=\ln\sqrt{u^2+1}+\ln(cx)=\ln\left(cx\sqrt{u^2+1}\right)'),eq(r'\tan^{-1}u=-\ln\left(cx\sqrt{u^2+1}\right)','iii'),paragraph('Back substitute u=y/x in equation (iii):'),eq(r'\tan^{-1}\frac{y}{x}=-\ln\left(cx\sqrt{\frac{y^2}{x^2}+1}\right)'),eq(r'\tan^{-1}\frac{y}{x}=-\ln\left(c\sqrt{y^2+x^2}\right)\quad\Rightarrow\quad\frac{y}{x}=\tan\left[-\ln\left(c\sqrt{y^2+x^2}\right)\right]'),note('The source logarithms implicitly choose a positive branch. A real local solution can be written ln√(x²+y²)+arctan(y/x)=C on x≠0, with a continuous angle branch. Retain x+y≠0 from the original equation. Since u²+1 has no real zero, no constant-ratio real solution is lost; u=−1 is excluded by the original denominator. The displayed tangent equation is still implicit in y; an explicit global formula is not produced. Curves may cross x=0 at y≠0 using a different local angle chart; x≠0 belongs to this substitution chart rather than the original equation.')]
REDUCTION=[heading('iii. Equations reducible to homogeneous form'),paragraph('A given differential equation of the form below, where a/a₁≠b/b₁, can be reduced to homogeneous form by taking new variables x and y such that x=X+h and y=Y+k, where h and k are constants chosen to make the given equation homogeneous.'),eq(r'\frac{dy}{dx}=\frac{ax+by+c}{a_1x+b_1y+c_1},\quad\frac{a}{a_1}\ne\frac{b}{b_1}'),paragraph('With the above substitutions dx=dX and dy=dY, hence dy/dx=dY/dX. Therefore:'),eq(r'\frac{dY}{dX}=\frac{a(X+h)+b(Y+k)+c}{a_1(X+h)+b_1(Y+k)+c_1}')]
REDUCTION_END=[eq(r'\frac{dY}{dX}=\frac{aX+bY+(ah+bk+c)}{a_1X+b_1Y+(a_1h+b_1k+c_1)}'),paragraph('Choose h,k such that ah+bk+c=0 and a₁h+b₁k+c₁=0. The differential equation becomes:'),eq(r'\frac{dY}{dX}=\frac{aX+bY}{a_1X+b_1Y}'),paragraph('This is a homogeneous equation.'),note('Use the division-free condition ab₁−a₁b≠0, which guarantees a unique translation and remains meaningful if a₁ or b₁ is zero. Keep the original denominator nonzero. When the determinant vanishes, this translation need not exist or be unique; a separate substitution or special-case analysis is required.')]
EX10=[heading('Example 10'),paragraph('Find the general solution of x dy/dx=x+y.'),heading('Solution'),paragraph('The given differential equation is not in the standard form of homogeneous differential equation, but can be reduced to it. Divide out by x:'),eq(r'\frac{dy}{dx}=\frac{x+y}{x},\quad\mathrm{HDE}','i'),paragraph('Use y=ux, dy/dx=u+x du/dx:'),eq(r'u+x\frac{du}{dx}=\frac{x+ux}{x}=1+u'),eq(r'x\frac{du}{dx}=1\quad\Rightarrow\quad du=\frac{dx}{x},\quad\mathrm{SDE}','ii'),paragraph('Integrating:'),eq(r'\int du=\int\frac{dx}{x}\quad\Rightarrow\quad u=\ln x+\ln c=\ln(cx)'),paragraph('Back substitute u=y/x:'),eq(r'\frac{y}{x}=\ln(cx)\quad\Rightarrow\quad y=x\ln(cx)'),paragraph('The general solution depends on a single arbitrary constant c.'),heading('Remember'),paragraph('If the differential equation is not homogeneous differential equation, then it might be a nonhomogeneous differential equation.'),note('On either x>0 or x<0, the general real solution is y=x(ln|x|+C). The source ln(cx) requires cx>0 and a nonzero c. The original x y′=x+y is already homogeneous in an implicit sense; division by x puts it into slope form. There is no C¹ solution on an interval containing x=0, because y′=ln|x|+C+1 diverges there. First-order homogeneous-in-ratio equations differ from the use of “homogeneous” for linear equations with zero forcing.')]
BACTERIAL_START=[heading('10.3.2 Solve real life problems related to differential equation'),heading('Example 11'),paragraph('A certain bacteria grows at a rate that is proportion to the number present at a particular time. If the number of bacterial at a time t=0 is N₀ and at time t=1 hour, the number of bacteria is 5N₀/2. Determine the time necessary for the number of bacteria to be quadruple.'),heading('Solution'),paragraph('If N(t) is the unknown number of bacteria w.r.t. time t hours, then the rate at which bacteria grows is:'),eq(r'\frac{dN}{dt}∝ N\quad\Rightarrow\quad\frac{dN}{dt}=kN'),paragraph('Reduce to separable form:'),eq(r'\frac{dN}{N}=k\,dt','i'),paragraph('Integrate to obtain the general solution:'),eq(r'\int\frac{dN}{N}=\int k\,dt'),eq(r'\ln N=kt+c','ii')]
BACTERIAL_END=[paragraph('The initial condition N(0)=N₀ is used in equation (ii) to obtain c:'),eq(r'\ln N_0=0+c\quad\Rightarrow\quad c=\ln N_0','iii'),paragraph('Use c in equation (ii) to obtain a particular solution:'),eq(r'\ln N=kt+\ln N_0\quad\Rightarrow\quad\ln\frac{N}{N_0}=kt\quad\Rightarrow\quad\frac{N}{N_0}=e^{kt}\quad\Rightarrow\quad N=N_0e^{kt}','iv'),paragraph('Use N(1)=5N₀/2 to obtain k:'),eq(r'\frac52N_0=N_0e^k\quad\Rightarrow\quad e^k=\frac52\quad\Rightarrow\quad k=\ln\frac52=0.9163','v'),paragraph('Use k in equation (iv) to obtain a particular solution:'),eq(r'N=N_0e^{0.9163t}','vi'),paragraph('The condition N=4N₀, when bacteria have quadrupled, obtains the time:'),eq(r'4N_0=N_0e^{0.9163t}\quad\Rightarrow\quad4=e^{0.9163t}\quad\Rightarrow\quad0.9163t=\ln4\quad\Rightarrow\quad t=\frac{\ln4}{0.9163}=1.51\ \mathrm{hr}'),paragraph('At this time the bacteria is four times the original number.'),note('Assume N₀>0 and use k=ln(5/2) without rounding until the final answer: t=ln4/ln(5/2)≈1.5129416 hours. The source four-decimal k is an approximation. This is the stipulated exponential model; its constant per-capita rate is distinct from Example 6’s capacity-gap model.')]
TRAJECTORY_INTRO=[heading('10.4 Orthogonal Trajectories'),paragraph('Our experience with first order differential equations has taught us that such equations often have general solutions containing a single arbitrary constant. Each solution defines a corresponding set of integral curves. A nonempty set of plane curves defined by a differential equation involving just one parameter (single arbitrary constant) is commonly called a one-parameter family of curves. Of special importance in certain applications are those one-parameter families of curves which are orthogonal trajectories of one another.'),paragraph('The curves of a family F(x,y,c₁) are said to be orthogonal trajectories of curves of a family G(x,y,c₂), if and only if each curve of either family is intersected by at least one curve of the other family and at every point of intersection of a curve of F with a curve of G, the two curves are perpendicular.'),heading('10.4.1 Orthogonal trajectories of the given family of curves'),paragraph('Two families F(x,y,c₁) and G(x,y,c₂) are perpendicular at a point of intersection if and only if their tangents are perpendicular there. If tangent lines L₁,L₂ are perpendicular, the product of their slopes equals −1:'),eq(r'm_1m_2=-1'),paragraph('m₁ and m₂ are the slopes of the two tangent lines L₁ and L₂.'),eq(r'm_1=-\frac1{m_2}\quad\Rightarrow\quad\left(\frac{dy}{dx}\right)_G=-\frac1{\left(\frac{dy}{dx}\right)_F}','i'),paragraph('This is called the differential equation of orthogonal trajectories. If one family of curves F is given, the other family G can be found by solving equation (i).'),note('The negative-reciprocal formula applies when both slopes are finite and nonzero. Horizontal and vertical tangents are also perpendicular, handled using tangent vectors with dot product zero or by swapping the local dependent variable. Orthogonality requires regular intersections with defined tangents; singular points need separate treatment.')]
PAGES=[page('151_page_1.jpg',293,EX9+REDUCTION),page('151_page_2.jpg',294,REDUCTION_END+EX10+BACTERIAL_START),page('152_page_1.jpg',295,BACTERIAL_END+TRAJECTORY_INTRO)]
for p in PAGES:p.update(title='DIFFERENTIAL EQUATIONS',visualStatus='Complete native homogeneous derivation, translation conditions, bacterial growth and orthogonal slope definitions',alternateVerification={'pdf':'Math Book for 12 class KPTBB.pdf','pdfPage':(p['page']+9)//2,'side':'left' if p['page']%2 else 'right','reason':'Full calculations, logarithm signs, rates and initial values visually checked'})
EXAMPLES=[]
for n,q,steps,ans,pgs in [
('9','Solve y′=(y−x)/(y+x).',['Retain the domain x+y≠0. On a chart with x≠0, put u=y/x, so y′=u+xu′.','Then u+xu′=(u−1)/(u+1), hence xu′=−(u²+1)/(u+1).','Separate −(u+1)du/(u²+1)=dx/x. There is no real root of u²+1, so no constant-ratio solution is discarded.','Integrate: −(1/2)ln(u²+1)−arctan u=ln|x|+C.','Rearrange and substitute u=y/x to get ln√(x²+y²)+arctan(y/x)=K on a continuous local angle branch.','Differentiate the implicit result: (x+yy′)/(x²+y²)+(xy′−y)/(x²+y²)=0. Thus (x+y)y′=y−x, exactly the original equation.','The substitution chart excludes x=0, but the original equation permits x=0,y≠0; a continuous alternative angle chart extends a solution across such regular points.'],'ln√(x²+y²)+arg(x+iy)=K locally, with x+y≠0 and a continuous angle branch.',[293]),
('10','Solve xy′=x+y.',['Work on an interval with x≠0 and write y′=1+y/x.','Put u=y/x; differentiating y=xu gives y′=u+xu′.','Substitute: u+xu′=1+u, so xu′=1.','Integrate du=dx/x to obtain u=ln|x|+C.','Hence y=x(ln|x|+C). The source y=x ln(cx) is an equivalent branch parametrization where cx>0.','Check y′=ln|x|+C+1, giving xy′=x+y.','No differentiable solution extends through zero on an interval: its derivative diverges as x approaches zero.'],'y=x(ln|x|+C), x>0 or x<0.',[294]),
('11','Bacteria grow proportionally to their number; N(0)=N₀ and N(1)=5N₀/2. Find the quadrupling time.',['Assume N₀>0, with t measured in hours, and form N′=kN.','Separate dN/N=k dt and integrate ln N=kt+C.','Use N(0)=N₀: C=ln N₀, hence N(t)=N₀e^(kt).','Use N(1)=5N₀/2: e^k=5/2, giving k=ln(5/2) per hour.','At quadrupling, N(t)=4N₀ implies e^(kt)=4 and kt=ln4.','Therefore t=ln4/ln(5/2)≈1.5129416 hours, or about 1.51 hours.','Verify N′=kN, N(0)=N₀ and N(1)=2.5N₀. Keep k exact until final rounding.'],'t=ln4/ln(5/2)≈1.51 hours.',[294,295])]:
 EXAMPLES.append({'num':n,'title':'Homogeneous Equations and Growth','question':q,'steps':steps,'answer':ans,'progressiveSolution':True,'solutionOrigin':'SpaceBook learning enhancement','sourceReferences':[{'page':pg,'jpg':f'{(pg+9)//2:03d}_page_{1 if pg%2 else 2}.jpg','verification':'Full source calculation and conditions visually checked'} for pg in pgs]})
